  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 212 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThe upper level pattern becomes less amplified through the \ncourse of the week as the western ridge breaks down and the \ndeparting system ejects northeastward into the eastern Canadian \nProvinces. The axis to the western ridge attempts to shift east \ninto the Mississippi Valley but de-amplifies as it does so. The \nend result is a subtle day-to-day warming trend with no strong \ntemperature advection to account for through at least Thursday. \n\nBy midweek, upper ridging attempts to reestablish itself over \nthe east coast with southerly return flow drawing warm, moist \nair back into the central U.S. Meanwhile, a surface low over \nCanada occludes near the Hudson Bay at the eastern edge of an \nupper level, longwave trough. This system sends a cold front \nsouthward into the central U.S. Wednesday into Thursday. Ahead \nof the front, a narrow envelop of dry air remains around the \ninverted surface ridge, but gradually gives way to increasing \nmoisture. Global guidance is in decent agreement with the \noverall synoptic pattern, but differs in placement and timing of \nsmall scale features. \n\nThere is some semblance of a surface system over the central \nPlains that rides along the front Wednesday night into Thursday.\nThis, along with subtle timing/placement differences in the \nfront will impact precipitation chances, but the general trend \nwill be toward showers and thunderstorms as the front \napproaches, and especially if the disturbance over the central \nU.S. becomes any better organized. LREF IQR spreads are greatest\nalong the front, which is positioned over Iowa Wednesday night.\nBy Thursday, these spreads narrow quite rapidly along with the \nreduction in overall precipitation amounts. These trends may be \na telling sign of a stalling front as upper and lower IQR\n6-hourly precipitation data falls below 0.05. This seems \nplausible with the build of the eastern ridge (southerly push \nover the Midwest) and the front's increasing departure from \nit's origin (Canadian low). That being said, should the front \ndrop farther south and stall, it may become a focusing mechanism\nfor additional disturbances. Considering that PWATs near 2 \ninches showers and thunderstorms could be efficient rain-\nproducers should support materialize. This will be something to\nmonitor to fine tune the placement of heavy rain potential\nthrough late week into next weekend.\n\nDaily highs stair-steup upward through the 80s this week with \n90s possible by Friday into next weekend. Low temperatures in \n60s give way to warmth and lows in 70s expand across the region\nbetween Wednesday and Friday nights.",
